---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Agentic Looping"
  description="Step back and let Claude run its own loop — observe, decide, act, repeat — until the goal is met. Plus the OODA-loop mindset and community tools for designing loops."
  pageId="agentic-looping"
  readMinutes={8}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🤖"
    title="Agentic Looping"
    subtitle="If manual looping is you having a back-and-forth with Claude, agentic looping is you stepping back and letting Claude have that conversation with itself — automatically, repeatedly, until the work is done."
    readMinutes={8}
  />

  <Callout variant="chat" title="🔁 New here?">
    <p>
      Start with <a class="text-chat underline" href={href('/manual-looping/')}>Manual Looping</a>
      — it's the everyday skill this builds on. Understanding the manual version
      makes the agentic version click instantly.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      An agentic loop gives Claude a goal, lets it act, evaluates the result,
      feeds that back in, and repeats — until the goal is met. You define the
      goal and the rules, then let it run. The competitive edge isn't any single
      output; it's the <strong>cycle rate</strong>.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <figure class="my-6 rounded-r-[12px] border-l-[3px] border-cowork bg-cowork/5 px-5 py-4">
    <blockquote class="m-0 text-lg italic leading-relaxed text-text">
      "I don't prompt Claude anymore. I have loops running. They're the ones
      prompting Claude and figuring out what to do. My job is to write loops."
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-cowork">
      — Boris Cherny, Head of Claude Code, Anthropic
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- What is an agentic loop -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>What is an agentic loop?</h2>
    <p>An agentic loop is a system that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Gives Claude a goal,</li>
      <li>Lets Claude take an action toward that goal,</li>
      <li>Evaluates the result,</li>
      <li>Feeds that result back into Claude as the next input,</li>
      <li>and repeats until the goal is met.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Instead of responding once, Claude acts, observes the result, decides what
      to do next, and repeats. The human isn't in the loop for every step — you
      define the goal and the rules, then let the system run. If that structure
      sounds familiar, it should.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- OODA -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>The OODA loop connection</h2>
    <p>
      Military strategists and fighter pilots have run agentic loops for decades —
      they just call it something different. Colonel John Boyd developed the
      <strong>OODA Loop</strong> for decision-making under pressure:
      <strong>Observe, Orient, Decide, Act</strong> — then cycle back and repeat,
      faster than your adversary. Agentic AI loops follow the exact same logic:
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-[12px] border border-border">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-border bg-card-hover">
          <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-cowork">OODA</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-cowork">Agentic Loop</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-cowork">Observe</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Claude reads the current state — a document, a dataset, an error message.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-cowork">Orient</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Claude evaluates what it sees against the goal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-cowork">Decide</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Claude determines the next best action.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-cowork">Act</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Claude executes — writes, edits, queries, calls a tool.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <section class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Then it loops. Immediately. Boyd's insight was that <em>speed through the
      loop</em> — not raw firepower — creates decisive advantage. The same applies
      here: a loop running overnight can complete work that would take a person
      days of back-and-forth. The advantage isn't in any single output — it's in
      the cycle rate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Example -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>A real-world example</h2>
    <p>
      At Anthropic's Code with Claude 2026 conference, live demos showed
      autonomous agents tackling real GitHub issues in production codebases —
      reproducing bugs, proposing fixes, running tests, iterating, and preparing
      pull requests with minimal human intervention. The developer didn't prompt
      step by step; they defined the task, set the parameters, and Claude cycled
      through its own OODA loop until the job was done.
    </p>
    <p>
      For Aptera, the same principle travels. Imagine a loop that monitors
      incoming vendor documents, extracts the relevant data, checks it against a
      template, flags discrepancies, and drafts a response — all without anyone
      prompting each step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Key ingredients -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>The key ingredients</h2>
    <p>A well-designed agentic loop needs three things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A clear goal</strong> — Claude needs to know what "done" looks like.</li>
      <li><strong>A feedback mechanism</strong> — each output gets evaluated and fed back in as input.</li>
      <li><strong>A stop condition</strong> — a loop without one is a liability; Anthropic's own guidance lists stopping conditions like a maximum number of iterations as a core control.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="code" title="⚠️ The non-negotiable">
    <p>
      Without that third ingredient, you don't have a productivity tool — you
      have a runaway process.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Design the loop -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Design the loop before you run it</h2>
    <p>
      A loop is only as good as its design. A sloppy one produces weak output —
      and worse, it can quietly burn through a lot of
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/tokenomics/')}>tokens</a> before
      anyone notices. Two ideas from the broader Claude community are worth
      borrowing:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Prove "done" — don't assume it.</strong> The strongest loops
        demand <em>evidence</em> before calling a task complete — a passing test,
        a regression check, a measurable margin — rather than trusting the model's
        own say-so. That "show your proof" discipline is the backbone of the loops
        in the <a class="text-gold underline" href="https://signals.forwardfuture.ai/loop-library/">Loop Library</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Don't let a loop grade its own homework.</strong> When the "are we
        done?" check is judged by the same model doing the work, that's a blind
        spot. A simple fix is a review council — have a <em>different</em> model
        evaluate whether the goal was actually met. This insight comes from Kevin
        Simback's <a class="text-gold underline" href="https://github.com/ksimback/looper">Looper</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="✅ A quick readiness test">
    <p>
      Before you run a loop, make sure you can answer two questions: "What proof
      tells me this is done?" and "Who checks the work?" If you can't answer both,
      the loop isn't ready yet.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Who is this for -->
  <section class="prose-content">
    <h2>Who is this for at Aptera?</h2>
    <p>
      You don't need to know how to code to build an agentic loop. Claude knows
      how to code — that's the point. Using Claude Code, you can
      <strong>describe the workflow you want in plain English</strong>, and Claude
      can build the loop for you — writing the scripts, setting up the logic, and
      configuring the stop conditions. The skill you bring is understanding your
      own process well enough to describe it clearly.
    </p>
    <p>
      A great first step before building anything is to
      <strong>have a conversation with Claude about your idea</strong>. Describe
      the repetitive task. Ask whether it's a good candidate, where the edge cases
      are, what the stop conditions should be, and whether a simpler
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/manual-looping/')}>manual loop</a>
      might serve you better. Think of it as a free consultation before you commit.
    </p>
    <p>Some roles where agentic loops make particular sense:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>SW Engineering</strong> — automated code review, test generation, issue triage.</li>
      <li><strong>Finance / Ops</strong> — document processing, report generation, AP workflows.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacturing / Quality</strong> — procedure validation, checklist generation, supplier document review.</li>
      <li><strong>CAD / Engineering</strong> — change-order summarization, BOM cross-referencing, documentation updates.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you're not sure where to start — that's what IT is for. Bring your idea and we'll figure it out together.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Resources -->
  <section>
    <h2 class="mb-1 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Resources: go deeper</h2>
    <p class="mb-5 text-muted">
      Two community-built tools that pair perfectly with this lesson — one for
      grabbing loops that already work, one for designing your own the right way.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <a
        href="https://signals.forwardfuture.ai/loop-library/"
        class="group rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5 transition-colors hover:border-gold/50 hover:bg-card-hover"
      >
        <h3 class="m-0 text-lg font-bold text-text group-hover:text-gold">📚 Loop Library</h3>
        <p class="mt-0.5 mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-dim">Forward Future · shared by @MatthewBerman</p>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A curated, copyable library of 30+ ready-to-run agent loops across
          engineering, operations, evaluation, content, and design. Every loop
          ships with built-in checks, evidence requirements, and clear stopping
          conditions.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 m-0 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">signals.forwardfuture.ai/loop-library →</p>
      </a>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/ksimback/looper"
        class="group rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5 transition-colors hover:border-gold/50 hover:bg-card-hover"
      >
        <h3 class="m-0 text-lg font-bold text-text group-hover:text-gold">🛠️ Looper</h3>
        <p class="mt-0.5 mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-dim">Open source · by Kevin Simback (@KSimback)</p>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A loop <em>design</em> coach for Claude Code. Run <code>/looper</code>
          and it interviews you, sharpens your goal, builds a verification rubric,
          recommends a different model as an independent judge, sets guardrails and
          a token budget, and hands you a portable, repeatable loop.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 m-0 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">github.com/ksimback/looper →</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="💡 Have an idea?">
    <p>
      Not sure if your process is a good candidate for a loop? Start by asking
      Claude — then reach out to IT when you're ready to build.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>An agentic loop gives Claude a goal, lets it act, evaluates, and repeats — you set the rules, then step back.</li>
      <li>It's the OODA loop: the advantage is the cycle rate, not any single output.</li>
      <li>Every loop needs a clear goal, a feedback mechanism, and a stop condition — the stop condition is non-negotiable.</li>
      <li>Design before you run: prove "done" with evidence, and don't let a loop grade its own homework.</li>
      <li>You don't need to code — describe the workflow to Claude, and lean on IT to help you build.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
